### 6\. Mathematical quorum integrity

One of the most important properties is quorum intersection.

For , two quorums of size must intersect in at least:

(2f+1)+(2f+1)-(3f+1)=f+1

Since at most validators are Byzantine, at least one validator in the intersection must be honest.

This supports the safety argument that two conflicting committed decisions cannot both obtain valid quorums, provided honest validators follow the protocol and never commit conflicting values under the same consensus instance.

Three properties must be maintained:

* Safety: Honest validators do not commit conflicting states.

* Liveness: The system can eventually make progress under its synchrony and availability assumptions.

* Accountability: Provable protocol violations can be attributed and independently examined.

A timeout alone does not prove Byzantine behavior. Likewise, a valid quorum does not prove the truth of external Oracle data.

